RECORD OF PROCEEDINGS FOR THE GOVERNING BODY
OF THE CITY OF CHEYENNE
Office of City Clerk
March 14, 2011
The Governing Body of the City of Cheyenne met in regular session on this date
beginning at 6:00 p.m. in Council Chambers.  Present were: MAYOR - Richard L. Kaysen;
COUNCIL MEMBERS - Mr. Allen, Ms. Ash, Mr. Brown, Ms. Broyles, Mr. Pierson, Dr. Rinne,
Mr. Spiker and Mr. Valdez.  Absent: Mr. Collins.  Also present: Carol Intlekofer, City Clerk;
Barb Dorr, City Treasurer, and Dan White, City Attorney.  The pledge of allegiance was recited.
Consent Agenda. (All agenda items listed with the designation of [CA] are considered to
be routine items by the governing body and will be enacted by one motion.  There will be no
separate discussion on these items unless a member of the governing body so requests and
support by two other members is received.  Any item removed from the Consent Agenda will be
considered in its normal sequence on the agenda.)  Upon request by Mr. Brown, and pursuant to
support received by Dr. Rinne and Ms. Ash, agenda item #17 was removed from the Consent
Agenda.  Upon request by Dr. Rinne, and pursuant to support received by Ms. Ash and Mr.
Allen, agenda items #16, 22(b) and 22(d) were removed from the Consent Agenda.  Upon
request by Ms. Ash, and pursuant to support received by Dr. Rinne and Mr. Brown, agenda item
#23(a) was removed from the Consent Agenda. Mr. Brown moved to approve remaining
Consent Agenda items, seconded by Mr. Allen.  Motion carried. Voting “yes” -- all members of
the governing body present.
[CA]
Minutes from Regular Meeting of the Governing Body on February 28, 2011.  Motion
(per Consent Agenda) carried.
Mr. Brown declared a conflict of interest (business interest) due to a voucher payable to
Ameri-Tech HVAC Services, and vacated Council Chambers.  Procedural inquiry was made
concerning the process to divide the question.  Mr. Valdez moved to approve payment of
vouchers as presented, seconded by Mr. Allen.  Mr. Allen moved to divide the question to allow
for a separate vote on the voucher payable to Ameri-Tech HVAC Services, seconded by Ms.
Broyles.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” -- all members of the governing body present in Council
Chambers. Mr. Spiker moved to approve the voucher payable to Ameri-Tech HVAC Services,
seconded by Mr. Pierson.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” -- all members of the governing body
present in Council Chambers.  Following announcement of the vote, Mr. Brown returned to
Council Chambers.  Mr. Pierson moved to remove a voucher payable to J.R. Tree Experts from
the voucher payables listing, seconded by Mr. Brown.  Barb Dorr, City Treasurer, advised her
office was in receipt of an IRS (Internal Revenue Service) Notice of Levy against J.R. Tree
Experts, and, upon inquiry, Dan White, City Attorney, advised he had reviewed the Notice of
Levy and that it requires the City to pay whatever is owed to J.R. Tree Experts be payable and
submitted to the IRS. Mr. Brown raised a point of clarification on whether the motion, in
addition to removing it from the payables listing, should also include that it be voided with
payment of monies due issued to the IRS.  Ms. Dorr and Mr. White stated their agreement that
the motion should include wording that the voucher should be removed and voided, and that the
```

### Page 2 of 13

```text
City Treasurer is authorized to issue payment to the IRS.  Mr. Brown offered a friendly
amendment to Mr. Pierson’s motion to indicate the motion proposed is to remove the voucher
payable to J.R. Tree Experts from the payables listing; void the current voucher, and authorize
the City Treasurer to issue payment of monies due to the IRS, seconded by Mr. Pierson. Motion
carried.  Voting “yes” -- all members of the governing body present.  Main motion as amended
carried. Voting “yes” -- all members of the governing body present.
Mayor Kaysen opened a public hearing to receive comments and input regarding the
intent of the City of Cheyenne to submit an application for funding for the Wyoming Business
Council's Business Ready Community Grant and Loan, Managed Data Center Cost Reduction
Program, to assist in the establishment of a Data Center, funding electricity and broadband
expenses for three years.  Cindy Schneider, Grants Manager, briefly reviewed the purpose of the
proposed application, which is to assist Thought Equity Motion, Inc. to open a tier two data
center project in a portion of the former SafeCard Building on Pershing Blvd.  She discussed
elements of the data center project, which involves the Wyoming Business Council; local
benefits; an agreement involving the project that is on the current meeting agenda for referral to
the next Finance Committee meeting, and clarified the City’s role in serving as a sponsor for the
project, which involves no cost to the City.  She stated the State Loan and Investment Board
(SLIB) will have the final decision on approving the grant application, and acknowledged Mike
Emerson with Thought Equity Motion, Inc. and Tom Johnson, Regional Director, Wyoming
Business Council, were present to answer any questions.  There being no further comments and
purpose of the hearing having been met, Mayor Kaysen closed the public hearing.
“An Ordinance amending Section 5.12.060, Sales and Consumption--Restrictions, of
Chapter 5.12, Alcoholic Beverages, of Title 5, Business Licenses and Regulations, of the Code
of the City of Cheyenne, Wyoming, to authorize the issuance of a Malt Beverage Permit or
Catering Permit limited to malt beverages, with open container waiver, in conjunction with adult
softball/baseball games and tournaments at the Pioneer Park Baseball Complex.”  Major Dorr
expressed opposition to the ordinance, citing concerns regarding beer sales at a family oriented
activity; liability; 2009-10 statistical information from the Wyoming Association of Sheriffs and
Chiefs of Police involving alcohol related arrests and convictions.  Reporting for Finance
Committee, Mr. Valdez advised there was no recommendation on this item from Finance
Committee.  Mr. Brown moved to approve, seconded by Ms. Ash. Upon inquiry, City Clerk
Carol Intlekofer stated the definition of a malt beverage is provided in Wyoming Statutes,
adopted by the City, and that anything that can be purchased through a malt beverage distributor
meets the definition of a malt beverage.  Comment followed on other malt based beverages that
can be consumed in addition to beer under a malt beverage permit.  Ms. Ash moved to postpone
for 2 weeks (to March 28, 2011) for further review with regard to potential amendments to the
ordinance, seconded by Mr. Brown. Sharon Guerney encouraged Council members to consider
impacts of broadening malt beverage distributions and the social norms and environment within
the community, and suggested moving forward with action on the ordinance, as presented, at the
current meeting. Matthew Bowen spoke in favor of postponement to allow time for Council
members to evaluate the facts affiliated with the ordinance. Discussion included the following
topics: Opposition to postponement; support for postponement; postponement to allow
additional time to review proposed language changes to resolve some of the concerns that have
been raised; equality issues involving other City-owned sites where consumption and malt
```

