“An Ordinance repealing in its entirety, Chapter 8.44, Solid Waste, and creating a new
Chapter 8.44, Solid Waste, of Title 8, Health and Safety, of the Code of the City of Cheyenne,
Wyoming.” Matthew Bowen expressed his concerns with the City operating as a “roll-off
monopoly”; questioned the legal authority of the governing body; commented in support of
private entrepreneurs securing the right to provide roll-off container services; ordinance effect on
private businesses; use of authority; the need for clearly stated reasons for support/non-support
of the ordinance by members of the governing body, and questioned the need for the ordinance
to include wording regarding roll-off container services by private businesses.  John Felzien
questioned enforcement responsibility relative to violations of the ordinance and spoke in
opposition to the City having a monopoly in the roll-off business.  Upon inquiry, Dan White,
City Attorney, reviewed research he had conducted regarding municipalities’ authority to
exercise a monopoly relative to collection and disposal services of garbage and waste within
City limits. Jim Elias, Public Works Director, clarified that since the last City Council and
Finance Committee meetings, a meeting with private haulers had been held to discuss the issue
of roll-off containers being provided by private businesses, and that proposed language changes
to the ordinance have been generated for review by the City attorney for consideration as an
amendment, noting a 2 week postponement was requested at Finance Committee.  In response to
public inquiry, Mr. Elias stated the City’s roll-off provider requirements are to protect the health,
safety and welfare of citizens.  Mayor Kaysen clarified municipal government has the authority
to conduct business pursuant to Wyoming Statutes, and case law.  Vicki Nemecek, Assistant
Public Works Director, reviewed authority provided by Wyo. Stat. § 15-1-103, and language
contained in the ordinance allowing the City to make reasonable rules and regulations for
hauling refuse.  Upon further public inquiry, Mr. Elias and Mayor Kaysen provided information
on the cost to the City to haul solid waste to the landfill in Ault, CO, which helps extend the life
of the City’s landfill.  Frank Cole, developer, discussed his research of City Code wording
relative to roll-offs, and stated his belief that the City should not be in the roll-off business and
should allow private business to take it over.  Lee Bayne expressed concerns that additional
research should be conducted before third reading and commented that a more “dynamic way”
was needed procedurally for public engagement.  Mr. Valdez moved to approve on third and
final reading, seconded by Mr. Brown.  Mr. Valdez moved to postpone for 2 weeks (to March
28, 2011) to allow further discussion between staff and private haulers, seconded by Dr. Rinne.
Upon inquiry, Mr. Elias stated the meeting with the private providers of roll-off containers had
been productive and stated his belief that a consensus had been reached on proposed ordinance
```

