“An Ordinance De-Annexing from the City of Cheyenne, a parcel of land situate in a
portion of the south half of Section 29, T.14 North, R. 65 West, 6 P.M., City of Cheyenne,
th
Laramie County, Wyoming, containing 37.0 acres, more or less (located in the vicinity North of
Interstate 80, East of East Pershing Boulevard, and East of the KOA Campground area).”  John
Volk, Campstool Land Co., reviewed the property location involved in the petition to de-annex;
isolation of area; use of nearby properties; lack of nearby availability to City water and sewer
services; property ownership and zoning history, and future plans for the site if de-annexation is
approved which would involve County platting and possible covenants.  Reporting for Public
Services Committee, Ms. Ash advised there was no recommendation on this item from the
Committee.  Mr. Spiker moved to approve on second reading, seconded by Mr. Valdez.
Discussion included: Concerns with de-annexing the property; regulations for City vs. County
zoning and development requirements, including landscaping; property and proposed future use
of the property if it is de-annexed, and area serving as part of the aesthetic gateway to that side
of the community.  Upon inquiry, Matt Ashby, Planning Services Director, discussed differences
between City and County zoning classifications and uses allowed; recently adopted new County
subdivision regulations with landscaping requirements less than what is required in the City, and
water and sewer requirements relative to use of the property.  Upon inquiry, Mr. Volk stated his
company would create property covenants, stating typically covenants address such things as
exterior building types, fencing, structure height; advised City Code allows for a request for
landscaping variances through the Board of Adjustment, and noted the biggest issue is providing
restrooms on the property.  Motion carried.  Voting “yes” -- all members of the governing body
present with the exception of Ms. Ash, Ms. Broyles and Mr. Pierson voting “no”.
